Z||000003||DXR_DAD||08-05-2001||05:19 PM||||MD, it's been done. Use the search (Right Side Top of the page)  Use intake, cold air, filter, any of these words, in the tech section. You will get more info than you need on making  the system  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Many members here have done it.<P>I hope this helps<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000004||Formula88||08-06-2001||09:08 AM||||Also, consider why you're doing a CAI.  Is it for more power or just better sound?  Remember, the Fiero has a cold air intake from the factory.  The air intake is plumbed to the fender vent so it gets only outside air.  Tests have shown there's not much, if any, improvement to be had over the stock system.  On mine, I just removed the water diverter box in the fender and replace the tubing with a flexible intake tube that runs from the vent to the base of the factory air cleaner housing.  I get the cool intake sounds and still have the ease of changing my filter from the engine compartment, not the fender.  Total cost, about $20.||||reg||6
Z||000005||MD_Fiero||08-06-2001||12:55 PM||||Well, looks really =)... i could care less right now, come on man, its a 4 cyl =) lol...i just want it to look good...gotta get some red spark plug wirez...etc...since my car is red..in bout 7-8 months ill be byeing a rice burner =) Honda  DelSol to be exact.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||08-06-2001||01:03 PM||||I don't know of anyone making a CAI for the Duke, but you could just put an open element air cleaner on it if all you are interested in is looks.  That'll make it sound better too.  Either that, or custom make your own with plumbing to the fender vent.  For it to be a true CAI, it has to breathe outside air.  The stock system already does that.  You could paint/chrome the air cleaner and then run some polished pipe to the fender vent, or painted PVC, or whatever.  You're only limited by your imagination.||||reg||6
Z||000007||MD_Fiero||08-06-2001||01:12 PM||||allright, thanks =)..once i get the money, should be very soon, 2 weeks really, ill start on that....do u think it will be an all day'er sorta thing?, or like just an hour or so?..thanks again||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||08-07-2001||12:06 AM||||I made one that's hidden in the wheelwell,just above the vent,so you may want to do something else if you just want it for looks,but here's how I did it.<BR>To keep the element dry I made a little baffle out of a piece of 3/16" thick plastic sheet(ABS,or Cintra)to keep the rain from getting on the filter.This covers the opening and is about 2" back in the wheelwell.<BR>I used a stock V6 element,and a 4" sewer cap trimmed down to where it's just a flat thing,a PVC 4" to 3" reducer,and a molded rubber intake hose from a Buick 3.1 that had the temp sensor built into it.<BR>To hold it all together,I used 1/4" threaded rod through the reducer,across the 4" opening,attached with nylocks on each side,then bent another piece of the 1/4" threaded rod into a hook at one end,hooked the onto the first piece,straight out through the center of the cap,attached using a nylock nut.<BR>Took less than an hour to make,looks stock,not much $,and uncorked my 3.4.||||reg||1
